Rip Esselstyn had me speak

115
00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.070
at the whole Whole Foods Market headquarters

116
00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.870
in 2010, I believe.

117
00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.410
My book had just come out

118
00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.460
and, yeah, I was traveling in my Prius

119
00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:18.110
and just sleeping in my car

120
00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:19.280
unless I had a friend to crash with

121
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.770
and that's what I was trying to do

122
00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:22.910
just to get off the ground.

123
00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:25.018
Right, well, a lot has changed.

124
00:04:25.018 --> 00:04:25.851
(chuckles)

125
00:04:25.851 --> 00:04:26.684
A lot has changed.
In the last year.

126
00:04:26.684 --> 00:04:28.010
We're in a very different moment

127
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:33.000
and I suspect both of you are gratified by the cultural tilt

128
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.450
in the direction of things that you guys care about

129
00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.790
and have been talking about for a long time.

130
00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.000
Yeah, absolutely.

131
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.500
It's so, so different

132
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.100
to be plant-based athlete these days

133
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.390
than even five or 10 years ago, just so different.

134
00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:48.650
Yeah.

135
00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:49.810
In many good ways.

136
00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.450
It's also a lot easier to get the junk food.

137
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:54.740
It doesn't mean quite the same thing to be vegan anymore,

138
00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.040
but, yeah, it's reaching so many more people.

139
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.316
Yeah, that's crazy.

140
00:04:58.316 --> 00:04:59.149
So, we're very grateful.

141
00:04:59.149 --> 00:05:01.340
And that's the right word, Rich, is grateful.

142
00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.080
We have that gratitude.

143
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.770
We were believing in something a long time ago,

144
00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.400
hoping that it would take off,

145
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.770
hoping that it would strike a chord with people,

146
00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:11.050
hoping people would embrace it

147
00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.410
and obviously people like you and others

148
00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.420
have given us a bigger platform.

149
00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.300
Yeah.

150
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.133
It's amazing.

151
00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:20.380
I want this to be really instructive for the audience

152
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.650
with a lot of kind of tactics and strategies

153
00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.260
that will be helpful to people

154
00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.760
that are all lifted from your amazing new book,

155
00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:29.670
which we're gonna get into

156
00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:30.650
but I think it would be good

157
00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.200
to just introduce yourselves a little bit.

158
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.740
I wanna spend a couple minutes

159
00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.950
on each of your respective backgrounds

160
00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.160
'cause I think that helps contextualize everything

161
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:39.993
that we're gonna talk about.

162
00:05:39.993 --> 00:05:41.750
So, Robert, we can start with you.

163
00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:45.640
I know you grew up on, basically, a farm in Oregon, right?

164
00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.090
And at a very early age made this shift.

165
00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.340
Yeah, I grew up on a farm in Western Oregon, Corvallis,

166
00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.890
Oregon State University town.

167
00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:57.220
My father was a professor at Oregon State University.

168
00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:58.140
That's where he met my mother.

169
00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.530
He taught animal agriculture, right?

170
00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:00.530
Yeah, animal science.

171
00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:03.860
He actually is or was at the time, he's retired now,

172
00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:07.420
but is a world expert on raising animals for food.

173
00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:08.600
So, when I was a young kid,

174
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.100
he traveled to every continent except for Antarctica.

175
00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.970
He wrote 15 textbooks for college students

176
00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.930
about raising animals, particularly raising rabbits.

177
00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:18.690
And so, he would travel around--

178
00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.220
Raising rabbits for food?

179
00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.290
Yeah, so, he would go around

180
00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:22.760
to different countries around the world

181
00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.690
teaching various students or cultures

182
00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.660
about the efficiency of raising rabbit meat.

183
00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.440
He even won a rabbit Oscar in the '80's, I think,

184
00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.600
as a top rabbit researcher in the world

185
00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.580
and wrote a bunch of textbooks.

186
00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:39.300
And that's the background that I came from.

187
00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.240
I used to raise rabbits

188
00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.800
and I would show them in the 4-H County Fair

189
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.191
and sell them at the auction

190
00:06:44.191 --> 00:06:47.110
and I did that with dairy cows, with chickens.

191
00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.020
And I came from this farming background

192
00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.600
and not only that but that animal science background,

193
00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.480
where both of my parents were animal scientists

194
00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.030
and were very concerned that in 1995 when I became vegan

195
00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:00.640
really out of respect for my older sister,

196
00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.210
who was organizing an animal rights week at our high school,

197
00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.820
that I wasn't gonna get enough, proper nutrition.

198
00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.800
This was slightly before the internet, 1995,

199
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.800
or weeks before the public internet.

200
00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:12.810
There was a lot of fear

201
00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.470
that you're just not gonna get enough protein

202
00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:17.830
and you're gonna be malnourished on top of that.

203
00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:20.320
I think you know part of my story.

204
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.420
When I was in eighth grade, I weighed 89 pounds.

205
00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:26.200
I was up to the massive 120

206
00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.150
by the time I was a sophomore in high school

207
00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:28.983
and became vegan.

208
00:07:28.983 --> 00:07:30.000
I was a small guy.

209
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.833
Right.

210
00:07:30.833 --> 00:07:31.666
I was an endurance athlete.

211
00:07:31.666 --> 00:07:33.900
Well, (chuckles) let's hold on.

212
00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:35.390
I wanna back up a little bit.

213
00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:38.400
So, do you have other siblings besides your sister?

214
00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.233
Yeah.

215
00:07:39.233 --> 00:07:40.066
You do.

216
00:07:40.066 --> 00:07:40.899
Yeah, so my older sister, Tanya,

217
00:07:40.899 --> 00:07:43.440
became vegan vegetarian at a young age.

218
00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.610
Maybe she was eight or 10 or something.

219
00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.730
And then my younger brother, Ryan,

220
00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.263
was always the cowboy guy, always the farmer.

221
00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:54.980
Driving the big truck, gun rack on his pickup truck,

222
00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.527
bumper sticker is like "Enjoy a juicy steak tonight,"

223
00:07:57.527 --> 00:08:00.593
"Cowboys do it in eight seconds," all this stuff.

224
00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.370
And then my youngest brother, Clark,

225
00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.690
who was 11 at the time became vegetarian

226
00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:08.280
when I became vegan in December of '95.

227
00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.694
So, three of the kids in your family go vegetarian vegan.

228
00:08:12.694 --> 00:08:15.670
And this is a classic teenage rebellion

229
00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:17.290
against the ideals of the parents.

230
00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.230
Your parents must have lost their minds.

231
00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:20.370
Everybody lost their minds.

232
00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:21.930
Can you imagine the holidays?

233
00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.890
And my sister was especially outspoken.

234
00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:25.210
We would go to protests.

235
00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.150
We were involved in animal rights.

236
00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:28.450
Right, like Food Not Bombs.

237
00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.401
Exactly, we ran Food Not Bombs.

238
00:08:30.401 --> 00:08:31.234
(laughs excitedly)

239
00:08:31.234 --> 00:08:32.310
We ran Food Not Bombs in Corvallis.

240
00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.500
I was making soup for people in need

241
00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:35.780
or anyone who was hungry

242
00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.623
and serving it in central park in my town for years.

243
00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.518
So, rejecting the rabbit stew at dinner.

244
00:08:42.518 --> 00:08:44.490
Yeah, but also being 15

245
00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.190
and not knowing exactly what to eat

246
00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.970
and not having these resources, like you mentioned,

247
00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:51.020
Brendan Brazier and others, they weren't there.

248
00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:52.540
John Robbins had a book,

249
00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:53.680
there are a few other resources.

250
00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.910
Howard Lyman was big back then,

251
00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:56.743
but as an athlete--

252
00:08:56.743 --> 00:08:57.576
Peter Singer.

253
00:08:57.576 --> 00:08:59.520
Yeah, but as an athlete, it was man--

254
00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.353
Nothing.

255
00:09:00.353 --> 00:09:01.443
What do I do?

256
00:09:01.443 --> 00:09:02.276
And I was a five sport athlete in high school

257
00:09:02.276 --> 00:09:05.700
and had aspirations of doing something well after that.

258
00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:09.420
Right, so, it's basically a lot of tofu sandwiches--

259
00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:10.253
A lot of tofu.

260
00:09:10.253 --> 00:09:13.720
I even tried to get sponsored by a tofu company back before.

261
00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.600
I collected every package and saved them in my closet,

262
00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:17.530
which I know it sounds weird,

263
00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:22.180
but every package of tofu hotdogs and bologna and all that

264
00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:23.150
from Yves Veggie Cuisine

265
00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:24.490
and I saved hundreds of packages

266
