and all these people growing up

1583
00:56:14.766 --> 00:56:16.550
and then when she got into power lifting,

1584
00:56:16.550 --> 00:56:19.070
she almost set a world record in her very first meet,

1585
00:56:19.070 --> 00:56:21.360
where she weighs at 130 pounds

1586
00:56:21.360 --> 00:56:23.310
can pull over 400 pounds off the floor,

1587
00:56:23.310 --> 00:56:28.310
can squat 365 bench press, 250 or so and we trained together

1588
00:56:29.400 --> 00:56:31.730
and she uses 110 pound dumbbells in each hand,

1589
00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:33.340
pressing overhead.

1590
00:56:33.340 --> 00:56:35.730
We've laid press 1,000 together

1591
00:56:35.730 --> 00:56:37.400
and I'm almost twice her size

1592
00:56:37.400 --> 00:56:40.280
and she lists the same weight as I do

1593
00:56:40.280 --> 00:56:43.510
and she dominates every single sport she's ever been in.

1594
00:56:43.510 --> 00:56:46.240
She's been an elite champion at a world-class level

1595
00:56:46.240 --> 00:56:48.000
and she's totally humble.

1596
00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:49.720
She's under the radar.

1597
00:56:49.720 --> 00:56:51.470
She just quietly goes about her business

1598
00:56:51.470 --> 00:56:54.630
being pound for pound the best that that I've known.

1599
00:56:54.630 --> 00:56:57.376
Is she part of that vegan strong bodybuilding team

1600
00:56:57.376 --> 00:56:58.209
that you have?

1601
00:56:58.209 --> 00:56:59.042
Yes, she is.

1602
00:56:59.042 --> 00:57:00.510
And she's in the book, of course,

1603
00:57:00.510 --> 00:57:02.690
because she's just so fascinating.

1604
00:57:02.690 --> 00:57:03.796
Right.

1605
00:57:03.796 --> 00:57:04.629
And there's others too that are...

1606
00:57:04.629 --> 00:57:05.500
That's one of the fun things.

1607
00:57:05.500 --> 00:57:06.930
We can name drop.

1608
00:57:06.930 --> 00:57:09.220
You and Scott Jurek and some of these bigger names,

1609
00:57:09.220 --> 00:57:11.020
James Wilks, Rip,

1610
00:57:11.020 --> 00:57:14.940
but for me the fun part is telling the compelling stories

1611
00:57:14.940 --> 00:57:16.610
of those who are under the radar

1612
00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:18.400
and especially a lot of amazing women out there

1613
00:57:18.400 --> 00:57:20.670
who aren't in of the documentaries,

1614
00:57:20.670 --> 00:57:22.265
that aren't in books,

1615
00:57:22.265 --> 00:57:23.730
like Laura Kline,

1616
00:57:23.730 --> 00:57:27.853
who's dominating her sport as a world champion do athlete

1617
00:57:27.853 --> 00:57:32.000
and so many others in bodybuilding and other sports.

1618
00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:34.406
A bunch of Olympic athletes

1619
00:57:34.406 --> 00:57:36.080
that are just doing amazing things

1620
00:57:36.080 --> 00:57:37.160
and be able to share those stories.

1621
00:57:37.160 --> 00:57:42.160
Yeah, let's do a little myth busting,

1622
00:57:42.220 --> 00:57:45.414
starting off with the big one, the elephant in the room.

1623
00:57:45.414 --> 00:57:46.324
What could it be?

1624
00:57:46.324 --> 00:57:48.574
Yeah, what could this question possibly be?

1625
00:57:49.670 --> 00:57:51.010
Where do you get your protein?

1626
00:57:51.010 --> 00:57:52.860
Matt, you're a runner.

1627
00:57:52.860 --> 00:57:57.000
Robert, you're a power strength athlete.

1628
00:57:57.000 --> 00:57:59.573
Where do you get your protein?

1629
00:58:02.060 --> 00:58:03.780
We've given the same answer for the years and said

1630
00:58:03.780 --> 00:58:05.170
you don't need that much,

1631
00:58:05.170 --> 00:58:07.550
which I know is so unsatisfying to people.

1632
00:58:07.550 --> 00:58:09.410
I think they just want the secret source

1633
00:58:09.410 --> 00:58:10.870
so that we can all know how to do it

1634
00:58:10.870 --> 00:58:14.719
but I just don't think about it.

1635
00:58:14.719 --> 00:58:16.880
Even recently, I've gotten into kettlebell lifting

1636
00:58:16.880 --> 00:58:19.410
much more these days that than a runner

1637
00:58:19.410 --> 00:58:23.120
after kind of a prolonged lack of inspiration in sports

1638
00:58:23.120 --> 00:58:25.550
and just kind of doing the parenting thing instead

1639
00:58:25.550 --> 00:58:26.890
and entrepreneurship and all that

1640
00:58:26.890 --> 00:58:28.630
but I'm kind of back now

1641
00:58:28.630 --> 00:58:31.020
and really feeling great and having great results

1642
00:58:31.020 --> 00:58:33.660
and I don't take protein powder.

1643
00:58:33.660 --> 00:58:36.280
I eat beans fairly often.

1644
00:58:36.280 --> 00:58:38.220
I eat seitan now and then.

1645
00:58:38.220 --> 00:58:39.610
I eat tofu, which is beans,

1646
00:58:39.610 --> 00:58:42.937
but if you eat whole foods, it's there.

1647
00:58:42.937 --> 00:58:45.550
And the recent athlete studies are saying,

1648
00:58:45.550 --> 00:58:48.684
especially for vegetarian or vegan athletes,

1649
00:58:48.684 --> 00:58:50.210
and we can come out and say it,

1650
00:58:50.210 --> 00:58:53.780
you don't absorb plant protein, I think about 10% lower.

1651
00:58:53.780 --> 00:58:57.770
Sorry, you get a 10% less sort of efficiency in absorption.

1652
00:58:57.770 --> 00:58:59.590
So, you need a little bit more, for sure,

1653
00:58:59.590 --> 00:59:00.660
but it's not a ton more

1654
00:59:00.660 --> 00:59:03.560
and three quarters of a gram per pound of body weight,

1655
00:59:03.560 --> 00:59:04.823
that would be plenty.

1656
00:59:05.661 --> 00:59:08.420
The regular non-athlete recommendation

1657
00:59:08.420 --> 00:59:09.253
is much lower than that.

1658
00:59:09.253 --> 00:59:13.163
It's something like 0.36 grams per pound per day.

1659
00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:15.143
Really not that much.

1660
00:59:15.143 --> 00:59:17.230
And I think most people are eating way too much protein,

1661
00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:18.457
which I know we all say that,

1662
00:59:18.457 --> 00:59:20.257
and people don't seem to believe it.

1663
00:59:21.244 --> 00:59:23.120
And I think that's why the question perpetuates,

1664
00:59:23.120 --> 00:59:24.930
because there is no great answer like, oh yeah.

1665
00:59:24.930 --> 00:59:28.490
This is all you need to do to get that huge amount,

1666
00:59:28.490 --> 00:59:30.489
but you just don't need that much.

1667
00:59:30.489 --> 00:59:31.538
(chuckles)

1668
00:59:31.538 --> 00:59:33.070
That's what it is.
Robert?

1669
00:59:33.070 --> 00:59:36.420
Yeah, I think it's almost a separate episode.

1670
00:59:36.420 --> 00:59:38.140
This topic could just go on forever,

1671
00:59:38.140 --> 00:59:40.010
but I think it starts with,

1672
00:59:40.010 --> 00:59:42.710
why do you believe that you need a whole lot of protein

1673
00:59:42.710 --> 00:59:45.200
and what is your understanding of protein and what it does

1674
00:59:45.200 --> 00:59:47.390
and why are you obsessed with it?

1675
00:59:47.390 --> 00:59:49.060
I think it's for everyone to answer.

1676
00:59:49.060 --> 00:59:51.290
Why do we try to optimize our protein intake

1677
00:59:51.290 --> 00:59:53.230
without optimizing our carbohydrate intake

1678
00:59:53.230 --> 00:59:54.620
or optimizing our fat intake

1679
00:59:54.620 --> 00:59:56.465
or optimizing our sleep

1680
00:59:56.465 --> 00:59:58.690
or optimizing our recovery

1681
00:59:58.690 --> 01:00:01.780
or optimizing these other areas of performance?

1682
01:00:01.780 --> 01:00:03.340
Our hydration?

1683
01:00:03.340 --> 01:00:04.440
We don't.

1684
01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:05.273
We focus on protein.

1685
01:00:05.273 --> 01:00:06.106
I think it's been

1686
01:00:06.106 --> 01:00:08.630
the most brilliantly marketed thing around,

1687
01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:09.560
because what it's done

1688
01:00:09.560 --> 01:00:13.160
is it's convinced people who are not active, non-athletes,

1689
01:00:13.160 --> 01:00:15.940
it's convinced them that they need to reach for protein

1690
01:00:17.210 --> 01:00:19.520
in enriched food.

1691
01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:21.840
As a beverage when they're sitting on the couch

1692
01:00:21.840 --> 01:00:22.820
and watching television.

1693
01:00:22.820 --> 01:00:24.040
If they're gonna reach for something,

1694
01:00:24.040 --> 01:00:25.800
it's gotta be protein infused,

1695
01:00:25.800 --> 01:00:28.140
whether that's a meat product or a beverage

1696
01:00:28.140 --> 01:00:30.640
or a protein water or anything else,

1697
01:00:30.640 --> 01:00:32.280
because that's been conditioned that,

1698
01:00:32.280 --> 01:00:36.470
and even if people who are are quite overweight

1699
01:00:36.470 --> 01:00:38.540
or, clearly, we have consumed

1700
01:00:38.540 --> 01:00:39.900
a lot of protein in their life,

1701
01:00:39.900 --> 01:00:42.270
but they still are afraid that

1702
01:00:42.270 --> 01:00:44.230
they just need more of it

1703
01:00:44.230 --> 01:00:45.620
and that more is better,

1704
01:00:45.620 --> 01:00:48.850
but I'm convinced that that more isn't better

1705
01:00:48.850 --> 01:00:51.550
and it can have adverse implications on health

1706
01:00:51.550 --> 01:00:52.690
but also I think one thing

1707
01:00:52.690 --> 01:00:54.680
that people are totally missing here,

1708
01:00:54.680 --> 01:00:56.830
and I don't know if you've talked about this,

1709
01:00:56.830 --> 01:00:58.480
I'm sure with other with other guests,

1710
01:00:58.480 --> 01:01:02.600
but what happens when you emphasize protein intake?

1711
01:01:02.600 --> 01:01:04.460
To me, you completely overlook

1712
01:01:04.460 --> 01:01:07.450
the more nutrient rich complex carbohydrates

1713
01:01:07.450 --> 01:01:11.210
that provide more fiber, vitamins, minerals,

1714
01:01:11.210 --> 01:01:14.170
antioxidants, nitric oxide, water,

1715
01:01:14.170 --> 01:01:15.350
and all these other things

1716
01:01:15.350 --> 01:01:17.720
to optimize health or optimize performance

1717
01:01:17.720 --> 01:01:19.650
but we're focused on an isolated nutrient

1718
01:01:19.650 --> 01:01:21.670
that we often take in isolated form

1719
01:01:21.670 --> 01:01:23.790
and we miss out on all this other stuff,

1720
01:01:23.790 --> 01:01:24.980
because we're filling up on calories

1721
01:01:24.980 --> 01:01:27.240
that could have been complex carbohydrates,

1722
01:01:27.240 --> 01:01:31.433
which by the way, have a lot of protein in them too.

1723
01:01:33.290 --> 01:01:34.340
The way I think about it,

1724
01:01:34.340 --> 01:01:38.040
and let me know how this lands for you guys,

1725
01:01:38.040 --> 01:01:41.060
is you're correct, Robert.

1726
01:01:41.060 --> 01:01:42.850
We've been sold this narrative

1727
01:01:42.850 --> 01:01:45.750
that if you don't drink a protein shake

1728
01:01:45.750 --> 01:01:48.320
within 10 minutes of waking up in the morning,

1729
01:01:48.320 --> 01:01:50.990
that somehow you're not gonna be able to breathe air

1730
01:01:50.990 --> 01:01:52.080
in and out of your lungs.

1731
01:01:52.080 --> 01:01:53.683
That we're all walking around,

1732
01:01:54.700 --> 01:01:58.810
basically, in this constant state of protein deprivation,

1733
01:01:58.810 --> 01:01:59.900
but I don't know about you.

1734
01:01:59.900 --> 01:02:01.480
I don't know anyone who's gone to the hospital

1735
01:02:01.480 --> 01:02:02.930
for a protein deficiency.

1736
01:02:02.930 --> 01:02:04.220
I just don't think it's a thing.

1737
01:02:04.220 --> 01:02:05.230
I think you're right, Matt,

1738
01:02:05.230 --> 01:02:08.910
that almost everybody is far exceeding

1739
01:02:08.910 --> 01:02:12.620
their daily recommended intake of protein

1740
01:02:12.620 --> 01:02:14.070
but for some reason,

1741
01:02:14.070 --> 01:02:15.610
we've been spun this yarn

1742
01:02:15.610 --> 01:02:19.400
that we need copious amounts of protein to just live.

1743
01:02:19.400 --> 01:02:21.370
And I think it's a fallacy.

1744
01:02:21.370 --> 01:02:24.130
In truth when you look at food

1745
01:02:24.130 --> 01:02:27.360
and specifically plant-based foods,

1746
01:02:27.360 --> 01:02:28.240
when we're talking about protein,

1747
01:02:28.240 --> 01:02:30.120
we're talking about amino acids, right?

1748
01:02:30.120 --> 01:02:31.240
And we're really talking

1749
01:02:31.240 --> 01:02:33.520
about the nine essential amino acids

1750
01:02:33.520 --> 01:02:36.970
that our bodies can't synthesize on their own

1751
01:02:36.970 --> 01:02:39.660
and that we need to get from our food.

1752
01:02:39.660 --> 01:02:42.860
Those amino acids are the building blocks of protein.

1753
01:02:42.860 --> 01:02:45.410
But in truth, if you're grazing randomly

1754
01:02:45.410 --> 01:02:47.210
on plant-based foods,

1755
01:02:47.210 --> 01:02:51.570
it's almost like we've evolved

1756
01:02:51.570 --> 01:02:53.430
and the plant kingdom has evolved

1757
01:02:53.430 --> 01:02:55.580
to just automatically meet those needs.

1758
01:02:55.580 --> 01:03:00.070
You're gonna meet your 10% of calories from protein

1759
01:03:00.070 --> 01:03:04.157
just by eating plants of a relative spectrum

1760
01:03:06.070 --> 01:03:07.910
of plants throughout the day

1761
01:03:07.910 --> 01:03:10.370
and your body takes care of the rest.

1762
01:03:10.370 --> 01:03:11.380
It's just not a thing.

1763
01:03:11.380 --> 01:03:15.296
So, when you look at that number, what is it?

1764
01:03:15.296 --> 01:03:18.190
0.8 grams per kilograms,

1765
01:03:18.190 --> 01:03:20.100
kind of like quoted as the main thing,

1766
01:03:20.100 --> 01:03:23.750
I've calibrated my protein intake against that

1767
01:03:23.750 --> 01:03:25.407
and even when I'm training hard,

1768
01:03:25.407 --> 01:03:27.380
I'm still below that,

1769
01:03:27.380 --> 01:03:30.270
but I'm improving, I'm not getting injured,

1770
01:03:30.270 --> 01:03:31.700
I'm making gains

1771
01:03:31.700 --> 01:03:34.040
and it's often led me to question

1772
01:03:34.040 --> 01:03:36.100
this whole paradigm altogether.

1773
01:03:36.100 --> 01:03:38.350
Garth Davis is probably the most prominent person.

1774
01:03:38.350 --> 01:03:39.880
He wrote a whole book, "Proteinaholic"

1775
01:03:39.880 --> 01:03:41.950
on this subject matter.

1776
01:03:41.950 --> 01:03:45.250
We spend all this time worried about our protein intake.

1777
01:03:45.250 --> 01:03:47.440
Meanwhile, we're all fiber deficient.

1778
01:03:47.440 --> 01:03:50.510
We're all micronutrient deficient,

1779
01:03:50.510 --> 01:03:53.630
where we're not getting enough vitamin D or whatever it is.

1780
01:03:53.630 --> 01:03:56.380
We have all these other gaps in our diet

1781
01:03:56.380 --> 01:03:58.730
that should be causing us concern

1782
01:03:58.730 --> 01:04:02.250
and yet the focus always comes back to protein.

1783
01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:03.940
Well, let's continue a little bit further.

1784
01:04:03.940 --> 01:04:05.260
The short answer,

1785
01:04:05.260 --> 01:04:07.440
which I could have just given you, lead with,

1786
01:04:07.440 --> 01:04:09.820
is that if you consume adequate calories

1787
01:04:09.820 --> 01:04:11.030
based on your body's needs,

1788
01:04:11.030 --> 01:04:12.180
you'll get adequate protein.

1789
01:04:12.180 --> 01:04:13.470
You'll get enough protein.

1790
01:04:13.470 --> 01:04:14.303
That's the thing.

1791
01:04:14.303 --> 01:04:15.960
It's the calorie intake that's important.

1792
01:04:15.960 --> 01:04:18.473
So, people talk about, I gotta build muscle.

1793
01:04:18.473 --> 01:04:20.130
I've gotta put on mass.

1794
01:04:20.130 --> 01:04:23.050
Well, I've put on 100 pounds since I became vegan,

1795
01:04:23.050 --> 01:04:25.310
120 pounds to 220 pounds

1796
01:04:25.310 --> 01:04:27.560
and for the last decade,

1797
01:04:27.560 --> 01:04:29.670
I haven't consumed protein powders,

1798
01:04:29.670 --> 01:04:31.100
protein supplements, or even hadn't--

1799
01:04:31.100 --> 01:04:32.573
Right, that is what I want get into

1800
01:04:32.573 --> 01:04:34.540
because you've gone through this arc

1801
01:04:34.540 --> 01:04:36.530
in terms of your relationship with supplements.

1802
01:04:36.530 --> 01:04:38.561
Yeah, and with protein.

1803
01:04:38.561 --> 01:04:39.394
I can tell you,

1804
01:04:39.394 --> 01:04:40.860
I was consuming 300 grams of protein,

1805
01:04:40.860 --> 01:04:42.910
5,000 calories a day for a while.

1806
01:04:42.910 --> 01:04:45.730
Now I consume less than 100 grams a day

1807
01:04:45.730 --> 01:04:46.563
and 200--

1808
01:04:46.563 --> 01:04:47.473
Sorry to interrupt

1809
01:04:47.473 --> 01:04:48.490
but how much of that previously

1810
01:04:48.490 --> 01:04:50.590
was in the form of protein powder?

1811
01:04:50.590 --> 01:04:51.970
Six a day.

1812
01:04:51.970 --> 01:04:54.100
I worked for Vega for 10 years.

1813
01:04:54.100 --> 01:04:54.933
You should have seen.

1814
01:04:54.933 --> 01:04:56.350
I was like a GNC in my basement.

1815
01:04:56.350 --> 01:04:57.760
It was unbelievable.

1816
01:04:57.760 --> 01:04:59.790
I had access to anything I wanted

1817
01:04:59.790 --> 01:05:02.180
and I used it as long as it was plant-based.

1818
01:05:02.180 --> 01:05:03.013
I was doing, really,

1819
01:05:03.013 --> 01:05:05.650
sometimes five, six protein drinks a day

1820
01:05:05.650 --> 01:05:08.913
and I, actually, got bigger and stronger,

1821
01:05:10.010 --> 01:05:11.370
just overall better performance,

1822
01:05:11.370 --> 01:05:12.540
the best I've ever been.

1823
01:05:12.540 --> 01:05:13.680
Later in life,

1824
01:05:13.680 --> 01:05:17.480
late 30's, early 40's now a decade without

1825
01:05:18.380 --> 01:05:20.197
any of those sports supplements just vitamin B12.

1826
01:05:20.197 --> 01:05:21.570
Was it just curiosity,

1827
01:05:21.570 --> 01:05:22.830
or what was the impetus

1828
01:05:22.830 --> 01:05:26.338
to ditch that stuff and see what would happen without it?

1829
01:05:26.338 --> 01:05:27.171
(laughs lightly)

1830
01:05:27.171 --> 01:05:28.004
It came from when I worked

1831
01:05:28.004 --> 01:05:29.730
in the Forks Over Knives office

1832
01:05:29.730 --> 01:05:31.460
and I was down in Vega like crazy

1833
01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:32.350
and Brian and Robbie

1834
01:05:32.350 --> 01:05:34.520
were the only other guys in the office for a while.

1835
01:05:34.520 --> 01:05:36.410
And they were teasing me all the time.

1836
01:05:36.410 --> 01:05:38.180
Yeah, rabbits gotta have this.

1837
01:05:38.180 --> 01:05:40.100
Protein drink to tie his shoes

1838
01:05:40.100 --> 01:05:42.630
and another supplement to walk across the room.

1839
01:05:42.630 --> 01:05:44.270
They just kept on getting on me.

1840
01:05:44.270 --> 01:05:47.300
Here I was working for this film about whole foods

1841
01:05:47.300 --> 01:05:49.320
in reversing disease and being your best health

1842
01:05:49.320 --> 01:05:51.040
and all this stuff

1843
01:05:51.040 --> 01:05:52.660
and I was just sitting in the office

1844
01:05:52.660 --> 01:05:53.780
drinking powders all day long.

1845
01:05:53.780 --> 01:05:54.640
Right, but I get it

1846
01:05:54.640 --> 01:05:57.400
